# Untitled

## Sublimation-Vapor Phase Pseudomorphic Transformation of Template-Directed MOFs for Efficient Oxygen Evolution Reaction
Using vapor phase transformation to synthesize template-directed metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) shows great promise as an approach to avoid the shortcomings of solution-based strategies. However, among current research, either the products are confined to zeolitic imidazolate frameworks or the conversion technologies are limited to complex processes such as chemical vapor deposition. Here, a well-designed sublimation-vapor phase pseudomorphic transformation method is reported to fabricate vertically aligned nanosheet arrays of NiFe-based MOFs with a uniform and controlled thickness, derived from NiFe-layered double hydroxides. Benefiting from the optimized morphology and the high intrinsic activity originating from the synergistic coupling effect of NiFe metal clusters, the as-prepared MOF electrocatalysts displays a superior oxygen evolution reaction performance, requiring an overpotential of \(318\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(50\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) with a Tafel slope of only \(47\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\). Furthermore, a string of metal oxide-MOFs are obtained, demonstrating the universality of this strategy. By observing the different stages of transformation, the transformation and growth mechanism of MOF crystals is unveiled for the first time. These findings may inspire the exploration and preparation of more species of MOFs, further broadening their application areas.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have captured widespread interests over the past two decades as a novel class of porous
materials. Fundamentally, they are composed of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands, held together by strong coordinate bonds. Due to their structural specialty and tunability with high porosity and large surface area, they have the potential for applications in a variety of fields such as absorption, separation, catalysis, and drug delivery.[1–4] Owing to these particularities, in general, there are two strategies of exploiting MOFs: direct usage as active materials, or as precursors/templates to fabricate target products.[2,5] For example, MOF-based electrocatalysts,[6] MOF-derived hollow structural materials,[7,8] and MOF-derived carbon-based materials[9–11] have been developed as a substitute to costly noble metal catalysts in order to enhance the efficiency of oxygen evolution reaction (OER).
Thus far, almost all MOFs are synthesized by solvothermal methods.[9,12] In the last four years, an innovative template-directed strategy to fabricate MOFs in
solution has swept the academic community of the energy storage and conversion fields.[13–22] The templates can be fabricated from many different materials such as hydroxides,[13,14] carbonated hydroxides,[15–18] layered double hydroxides (LDH),[19,20] and metal oxides.[21,22] However, all these transformations suffer a series of problems like the crystallization of MOFs into 2D and 3D patterns that lead to the overoccupation of space between templates, heterogeneous nucleation of MOF particles like bicrystals in a narrow space, and insufficient conversions. In addition to compatibility with templates, the difficulty of solvothermal methods lies in precisely matching the template dissolution and MOF crystallization rates. These shortcomings finally limit the further improvement of the materials' performance. Additionally, the use of organic solvents opposes to the "green-synthesis" principles.[23] On the other hand, vapor phase transformation to synthesize MOFs is a promising method to circumvent these inherent drawbacks of solution-based strategies.[23–29] However, two issues strictly restricted the development of the vapor phase transformation method. One is that the mechanism of the transformation process is not clear yet. The other is that the ligand and precursor used for transformation are limited to 2-methylimidazole (2-MIM) and metal oxides/hydroxides, thus confining the products to mere zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs).
As for conversion technologies, they were limited to complex processes such as chemical vapor deposition and atomic layer deposition,\[23,24\] while the modified vapor-phase hydrothermal devices are only applicable to 2-MIM ligand.\[25–29\] Therefore, the wider applications of vapor phase transformation are hindered. How to obtain a wider variety of ligand gases by a simpler method, which also enable the vapor phase transformation of MOFs under the guidance of a clear mechanism, remains to be a great challenge.
Herein, we report a well-designed sublimation-vapor phase pseudomorphic transformation (SVPT) methodology to fabricate template-directed MOFs. By utilizing the simple customized apparatus and elaborately designed experimental protocol, LDH-derived NiFe-based MOFs nanosheet arrays (LDH-MOF NS) were fabricated with sublimated 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic (BDC) ligand gas. With the assistance of structural characterization, the transformation mechanism of LDH-MOF has been deeply explored and clearly revealed. The optimized electrocatalyst displayed outstanding OER activity in \(1\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH aqueous solution with an overpotential of \(318\,\mathrm{mV}\) at a current density of \(50\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\). Remarkably, the corresponding Tafel slope was as low as \(47\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1}\), which exceeded the values of the benchmark \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) catalyst and most of state-of-the-art electrochemical OER catalysts. It was unveiled that the outstanding electrochemical activity originated from the synergistic coupling effect of NiFe metal clusters. Furthermore, in order to demonstrate the universality of SVPT strategy, attempts have been undertaken to employ different metal oxides (NiO, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), and \(\mathrm{CuO}\)) to react with 2-MIM, BDC, and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic (BTC) ligands, respectively. As a result, a string of metal oxide-MOFs were obtained. Based on the observation of morphology change at the different stages of SVPT process, the transformation and
growth mechanism of MOF crystals, to the best of our knowledge, was drawn out for the first time.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Characterization of Nanostructure and Composition
As illustrated in Figure 1a, in situ SVPT synthesis of LDH-MOF NS mainly contains two steps. First, with the temperature rising, precursor NiFe-layered double hydroxide nanosheet arrays (NiFe-LDH NS) gradually converted to NiO and \(\mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanosheet arrays (NiFeO NS). Second, when the temperature reached the sublimation point of p-phthalic acid (PTA), the PTA powder sublimated into gas, leaving the outer template topologically transformed to LDH-MOF NS. Accordingly, the mechanism of SVPT could be described via the following equations (LDH-MOF is composed of NiFe-MOF and Ni-MOF)
\[
\mathrm{Ni}_{5,64}\mathrm{Fe}_{2,36}(\mathrm{OH})_{16}(\mathrm{CO}_3)_{1,18}\xrightarrow{350^\circ\mathrm{C}}\mathrm{NiFe}_2\mathrm{O}_4\cdot\mathrm{C}+\mathrm{NiO}\cdot\mathrm{C}+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}\uparrow+\mathrm{CO}_2\uparrow
\]
\[
\mathrm{NiFe}_2\mathrm{O}_4+\mathrm{PTA}\xrightarrow{350^\circ\mathrm{C}}\mathrm{NiFe}\cdot\mathrm{MOF}+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}\uparrow
\]
\[
\mathrm{NO}+\mathrm{PTA}\xrightarrow{350^\circ\mathrm{C}}\mathrm{Ni}\cdot\mathrm{MOF}+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}\uparrow
\]
The template NiFe-LDH NS on Ni foam (NiFe-LDH@NF) was characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Figure 2a; Figure S1a–d, Supporting Information), energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX, Figure S2a, Supporting Information), and X-ray diffraction (XRD, Figure S2b, Supporting Information). The sublimation point of PTA is below
\(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which was determined by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, Figure S3, Supporting Information). In addition, the SVPT process was conducted in a customized sealed quartz crucible that could provide an appropriate space to contain ligand gas (Figure 1b), thus greatly reducing the usage amount of ligand powder.
In order to obtain a homogeneously transformed LDH-MOF NS on Ni foam (LDH-MOF@NF), the mass of PTA, the transformation time, and the placement style of the precursor were elaborately designed and optimized. Horizontal placement (Figure S4, Supporting Information), improper mass (Figure S5, Supporting Information) and time (Figure S6, Supporting Information) would lead to unsatisfactory products. As shown in Figure 2b and Figures S7a and S8 (Supporting Information), after BDC ligand vapor treatment, vertically placed LDH-MOF@NF with \(7.5~\mathrm{mg}\) PTA mass and \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) reaction time (7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF) exhibited the ideal partial and overall transformation state, which perfectly inherited the morphology of LDH precursor with a uniform and controlled thickness, displaying excellent compatibility with the self-sacrificial template.
The keys to the success of SVPT are that (1) at appropriate temperature, the organic ligand powder sublimates; while the evaporation of product \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) facilitates the transformation (as shown in reaction mechanism equation); (2) the synthesized MOFs possess good thermal stability; (3) molecules/ions diffuse in a nanometer range within the reaction interface, thus simplifying and regulating the reactant transport and heterogeneous crystallization process.[23]
The morphology of control group NiFeO NS on Ni foam with 0-1h PTA mass-reaction time (0-1h NiFeO@NF, Figure 2c and Figure S7b, Supporting Information) demonstrates the topological conversion phenomenon of nanosheets during the SVPT process. XRD patterns of NiFe-LDH, LDH-MOF, and 0-1h NiFeO (Figures S9 and S10, Supporting Information) further indicate the transforming process as depicted in the schematic illustration.[30,31] NiFe-based MOFs, NiO, and \(\mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) ternary nanosheets were obtained as a result of the transformation process. Moreover, the sharp rise of carbon and oxygen atom percentages in the EDX spectrum (Figure S11a, Supporting Information) reflects the successful reaction of
vaporized substances and substrate. For comparison, \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) on Ni foam \((\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}@\mathrm{NF})\) with nanosheet morphology (Figure 2d) was selected as the second control group. Similarly, the SVPT approach enabled the conversion of hydroxide to \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) -MOF@NF (NiOH-MOF@NF) with a proper thickness (Figure 2e). Photograph, SEM, XRD, and EDX spectra results of template and \(\mathrm{NiOH-MOF@NF}\) (Figures S12–S14, Supporting Information) all provide the direct evidence of transformation. Considering the complexity of NiFe-based MOFs and the composition of LDH-MOF, NiO, and \(\mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) were separately synthesized as the precursors of MOF transformation for further analysis of NiFe-based MOFs (Figures S13 and S16, Supporting Information). The corresponding MOF products were named NiO-BDC and NiFeO-BDC. By comparing the XRD patterns of LDH-MOF, PTA, NiO-BDC, NiFeOBDC, and NiOH-MOF (Figure S17, Supporting Information), it could be concluded that the NiFe-based MOFs in LDH-MOF are composed of NiO-BDC and NiFeO-BDC that belong to Ni-MOF and NiFe-MOF \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}\) -MIL-88), respectively.[32–34] Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy was carried out to ascertain the functional groups of three powder samples. In Figure 2f, the absorption bands at 1556 and \(1384~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are ascribed to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations of the coordinated carboxylate group \(\left(-\mathrm{COO}^{-}\right)\) .[35] The value of \(\Delta=\nu_{\mathrm{asym}}(-\mathrm{COO}^{-})\ -\nu_{\mathrm{sym}}(-\mathrm{COO}^{-})\) is about \(172~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , which corresponds well to the bidentate bridging coordination mode of carboxylates and metal.[36] Moreover, the peaks at 1509 and \(748~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) correspond to the vibration absorption of benzene rings and \(\mathrm{C{-}H}\) bonding vibrations of the benzene rings.[37,38] In addition, the peaks of \({\approx}3630~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in NiOH-MOF spectrum and \({\approx}3400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in LDH-MOF pattern correlate with the stretching vibrations of \(\mathrm{O{-}H}\) from \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) and the \(\mathrm{O{-}H}\) stretching mode.[38,39] Thus, these results illustrate that the chemical bonds exist between the template and vaporized PTA. Another conclusion is that there are some carboxylate groups that remain uncoordinated and exposed on the surface of LDH-MOF, which may consequently elevate the hydrophilicity.[38,40]
To further demonstrate the structure of catalysts, high-resolution transmission electron microscope (HRTEM) was used for analysis. In Figure \(2\mathrm{g},\) there are two distinct groups of lattice fringes. The area circled by green dotted lines with a lattice distance of \(0.208\,\mathrm{\nm}\) corresponds to the (200) plane of NiO. The lattice distances of 0.208 and \(0.240\ \mathrm{nm}\) in the area circled by red dotted lines belong to the (400) and (222) planes of \(\mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4},\) respectively. The crystal planes' angle of the above planes is about \(54^{\circ}\) that is in good agreement with the theoretically calculated value. The broader lattice distance in the inset is about \(0.506~\mathrm{nm}\) , conforming to the (022) facet of MIL-88. The selective area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Figure 2h) indicates that the 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF has high crystallinity. The several diffraction rings belong to NiO and \(\mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) . Noticeably, the scanning TEM (STEM) and corresponding EDX mapping display four elemental distributions. The different enrichment degrees of Ni and Fe elements are inherited from the precursor, which is similar to 0-1h NiFeO@NF (Figure S18, Supporting Information). The thickness information gathered from height profiles of atomic force microscopy (AFM) characterization (Figure S19, Supporting Information) shows the thickness change after the transformation. Raman spectra of
7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF, LDH-MOF powder, and NiOH-MOF@ NF are shown in Figure S20 (Supporting Information). The two obvious bands at \({\approx}1590\) and \(1400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) of all three samples are associated with the in- and out-of-phase stretching modes of the carboxylate group. Another peak at \({\approx}640~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is assigned to the out-of-plane deformation modes of the \(\mathrm{C{-}H}\) groups.[38] These results prove the occurrence of the sublimation-vapor phase transformation. The chemical composition and surface valence states of 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF and control sample 0-1h NiFeO@NF were scrutinized by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). As illustrated in Figure S21 (Supporting Information), the materials contain O, O, Fe, and Ni elements, which are consistent with the results of EDX spectra and STEM mappings. In Figure S22 (Supporting Information), the difference between LDH-MOF and 0-1h NiFeO in high-resolution XPS spectra of O 1s and C 1s is due to the different connection modes of the corresponding elements. For 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@ NF, they are composed of the benzoic rings and carboxylate \(\scriptstyle(0-\complement=\complement)\) groups of BDC ligand, \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}\) bond, and \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{Ni}){-}\mathrm{O}\) bonds, and for 0-1h NiFeO@NF, there are only graphite C, \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}\) bond and \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{Ni}){-}\mathrm{O}\) bonds. For both species, the spectra of Fe and Ni can be deconvoluted into two peaks, attributed to Fe (Ni) \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Fe (Ni) \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) , respectively, due to the spin– orbit splitting.[30,38] This indicates the presence of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) . It is important to note that the slight shifts in the binding energy of Fe \(2\mathrm{p}\) and Ni \(2\mathrm{p}\) of 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF suggest a chemical coupling interaction between Fe(Ni) and the organic ligand. Furthermore, \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm (Figure S23, Supporting Information) demonstrates the superior porosity of 7.5-1h LDH-MOF.
## 2.2. Electrochemical Measurements
Oxygen evolution reaction is a promising application for MOFs. In order to investigate the OER catalytic activity of the obtained catalysts, bare Ni foam (NF), NiFe-LDH@NF, 0-1h NiFeO@NF, NiOH-MOF@NF, and LDH-MOF@NF transformed under various conditions were directly applied as the working electrode in a conventional three-electrode system in \(1\,\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH electrolyte. As a benchmark, commercial \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) on Ni foam \((\mathrm{RuO}_{2}@\mathrm{NF})\) was tested, using Nafion as a binder. Figure 3a shows the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) results. It is clear that Ni foam exhibits weak OER catalytic activity with extremely low current density. Two obvious oxidation peaks of NiOH-MOF@NF and NiFe-LDH@NF around 1.43 and \(1.50~\mathrm{V}\) correspond to the oxidation process of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\).[41] This explains why the polarization curves of these two catalysts rise earlier than 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF, while their catalytic performances for OER are inferior to it. Remarkably, 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF shows even better catalytic activity than commercial \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) with only \(275.38\,\mathrm{~mV}\) at \(10\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) and \(318.38\,\mathrm{~mV}\) at \(50\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) , comparing with \(320\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) at \(10\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) and \(395.5\,\mathrm{\mV}\) at \(50\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) of \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}@\mathrm{NF}\) (Figure S24a, Supporting Information). Additionally, the influence of the reaction conditions (ligand mass-reaction time) toward the OER activity of 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF was further explored. In accordance with the uniform nanomorphology and overall transformation state mentioned above, 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF exhibited the
best performance (Figure S25, Supporting Information). The superior OER electrocatalytic activity of 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF electrode outperformed other previously reported state-of-the-art OER electrocatalysts such as \( \mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Co} \)-Fe oxide DSNBs (\( \approx 340\;\mathrm{mV} \) at \( 50\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2} \)),[7] NiFeSe@NiSeO@CC (\( \approx 330\;\mathrm{mV} \) at \( 50\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2} \)),[17] \( \mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{EC} \)-MOF (\( \approx 325\;\mathrm{mV} \) at \( 50\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2} \)),[28] NC-MUV-3 (\( \approx 330\;\mathrm{mV} \) at \( 50\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2} \)),[29] and ER- \( \mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) NWs-2 (\( \approx 380\;\mathrm{mV} \) at \( 50\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2} \)),[42] as shown in Figure 3e. Apart from the overpotentials at specific current densities, Tafel slope is another important indicator that provides insight into the reaction kinetics for OER.[6,42] Figure 3b shows the Tafel plots of the corresponding LSV curves. The Tafel slope of 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF was as low as \( 47\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1} \), which was much smaller than the \( 124\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1} \) of the Ni foam, \( 83\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1} \) of the commercial \( \mathrm{RuO}_{2}@\mathrm{NIF} \), \( 74\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1} \) of the 0-1h NiFeO@
NF, and \( 70\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1} \) of the NiFe-LDH@NF. Such a low value indicates that the obtained 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF possesses intrinsically favorable OER activity and could reach high current densities at relatively low overpotentials, thus enhancing the OER efficiency and decreasing the superfluous energy consumption.
To further study the influence factors of catalytic kinetics, cyclic voltammetry (CV) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were conducted. As shown in Figure S26 (Supporting Information), 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF, 0-1h NiFeO@NF, NiFe-LDH@NF, and NiOH-MOF@NF were measured in a range of \( 0{-}0.1\;\mathrm{V} \) (vs saturated calomel electrode (SCE)) at a series of scan rates (ranged from 15 to \( 100\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1} \)). It is found that 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF exhibits the largest electrochemical double-layer capacitances (\( C_{\mathrm{dl}} \), \( 11.465\;\mathrm{mF}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2} \))
calculated from CV curves among obtained catalysts (Figure 3c; Figure S24b, Supporting Information). As there is a definite connection between electrochemical surface area (ECSA) and \( C_{\mathrm{dl}} \), the higher \( C_{\mathrm{dl}} \) suggests the larger ECSA.[13] Therefore, the number of accessible active sites of 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF is much higher than that of other catalysts. From the EIS Nyquist plot (Figure 3d), the charge transfer resistance \( \left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right) \) was only \( 4.2\ \Omega \) for 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF, but 13.1 and \( 44.5\ \Omega \) for 0-1h NiFeO@NF and NiOH-MOF@NF. The smaller \( R_{\mathrm{ct}} \) leads to a faster electron transport during OER, thus promising a higher OER activity.[15] Although the \( R_{\mathrm{ct}} \) value of NiFe-LDH@NF is the smallest, combining with the effects of ECSA, the 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF displays better OER performance. From the results of calculated TOF values (Table S1, Supporting Information), it could be concluded that the intrinsic activity of LDH-MOF is better than commercial \( \mathrm{RuO}_{2} \), thus displaying a better OER activity. Due to its superior intrinsic activity, the 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF surpass most of state-of-the-art electrochemical OER catalysts (Table S2, Supporting Information).
Aside from the catalytic activity, long-term stability is another crucial property for the practical applications of electrocatalysts.[16] 2000 cycles of CV scans were applied between \(-0.2\) and \(0.2\;\mathrm{V}\) (vs SCE) at a scan rate of \(50~\mathrm{mV~s^{-1}}\) . Afterward, the 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF was tested at \(10\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) in \(1\,\mathrm{~M~}\) KOH for \(10~\mathrm{{h}}\) , as shown in Figure S27a (Supporting Information). During the OER process, the overpotential kept stable with only a \(9~\mathrm{mV}\) increase at \(10~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}\) . At the same time, the LSV curves (Figure S27b, Supporting Information) before
and after chronoamperometry test demonstrate that the 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF maintained a steady OER activity. Additionally, after the stability tests, the morphology, elemental composition, and valence states of 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF were examined by SEM and XPS. The results hardly had any variations, implying the excellent stability (Figure S27c–f, Supporting Information).
It is obvious that the 2D nanosheet arrays' structure inherited from NiFe-LDH by SVPT and the porous nature of derived LDH-MOF contribute to the numerous exposed active sites and large ECSA. Moreover, these structural advantages may also be beneficial for charge transfer and mass transfer, thus endowing the catalyst with a good conductivity (small \( R_{\mathrm{ct}} \)) and magnificent stability.[40] However, NiOH-MOF@NF, 0-1h NiFeO@NF, and NiFe-LDH@NF also have some or all these advantages, so why are their catalytic activities not as good as that of LDH-MOF@NF? With the consideration of the Tafel slope and overpotential results mentioned above, it could be concluded that the intrinsic activity of active sites also plays an important role.[43] Generally speaking, the active sites of MOFs for OER are metal ions/clusters.[6,38,40] Therefore, when compared to the OER performance of NiOH-MOF@NF, it is apparent that the NiFe bimetal centers of LDH-MOF provide an enhancement to the OER catalytic activity. To gain profound insights into the electrocatalytic OER active sites, we further analyzed the electronic structure of Ni and Fe, which would affect the formation of hydroperoxy (OOH) species (key intermediates during OER). As illustrated in Figure 4, \( \mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4} \), NiO, and \( \mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) were separately transformed with
PTA by SVPT and measured by XPS (named as NiFeO-BDC, NiO-BDC, and FeO-BDC). The Ni and Fe spectra could be deconvoluted into two peaks, attributing to Ni (Fe) \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) and Ni (Fe) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\), respectively. Shifts to a lower binding energy of Ni (from 876.71 to \(876.03~\mathrm{eV}\) of Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) peak; from 859.30 to \(858.32~\mathrm{eV}\) of Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) peak) and a higher binding energy of Fe (from 725.79 to \(726.68~\mathrm{eV}\) of Fe \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) peak; from 712.36 to \(713.20~\mathrm{eV}\) of Fe \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) peak) could be clearly observed in NiFeO-BDC. According to the previously reported work,[38,44] such shifts are relevant to the modulation of Ni atoms' electronic structure induced by the introduction of Fe. A partial electron is transferred from Fe to Ni through the O atoms of the BDC ligand. The larger electron density can facilitate the formation of hydroperoxy,[6,45] therefore promoting activity using the synergistic effects of Ni and Fe. Interestingly, these effects, together with the uncoordinated carboxylate groups exposed on the surface of LDH-MOF nanosheets, may render LDH-MOF nanosheets extraordinarily hydrophilic, as suggested by contact angle measurement results (Figure S28, Supporting Information).[40] Consequently, the electrocatalytic performance of NiFeO-BDC is much better than NiO-BDC and FeO-BDC, as shown in Figure 4d.
## 2.3. Universality and Mechanism of SVPT
SVPT is a groundbreaking method to transform metal oxide (NiO), metal composite oxide \(\left(\mathrm{NiFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\right)\), metal hydroxide \(\left(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\right)\), and layered double hydroxide (NiFe-LDH) into metal/bimetal MOFs with PTA. To further demonstrate the universality of this extraordinary strategy, NiO, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), and \(\mathrm{CuO}\) were selected to react with PTA, trimesic acid (TMA), and 2-MIM, respectively. NiO, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), and \(\mathrm{CuO}\) with different nanomorphologies (nanosphere, nanocube, and nano-octahedron) were prepared in advance, characterized by SEM, EDX, and XRD (Figures S29 and S30, Supporting Information). The reaction temperature of TMA and 2-MIM was also determined by TGA to be 350 and \(180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), respectively (Figure S31, Supporting Information). The XRD patterns of TMA and 2-MIM were obtained to prove the products were not recrystallization ligands (Figure S32, Supporting Information). Similar to the reaction of NiFe-LDH and PTA, the transformation reaction of metal oxide (MO)-MOFs can be described as following equations (MO-MOFs are named as MO-BDC, MO-BTC, and MO-2MIM when the precursor MO reacts with PTA, TMA, and 2-MIM gases, respectively)
\[
\mathrm{MO}+\mathrm{PTA}\xrightarrow{350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}}\mathrm{MO}\cdot\mathrm{BDC}+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}\uparrow
\]
\[
\mathrm{MO}+\mathrm{TMA}\xrightarrow{350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}}\mathrm{MO}\cdot\mathrm{BTC}+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}\uparrow
\]
\[
\mathrm{MO}+2\cdot\mathrm{MIM}\xrightarrow{180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}}\mathrm{MO}\cdot2\mathrm{MIM}+\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}\uparrow
\]
Similarly, the good thermal stability of products MOF and the evaporation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) facilitate the transformation. By observing the morphologies of transformed MO-MOF crystals at different transformation stages, the growth mechanism of
MOF crystals formed by SVPT was proposed for the first time, as illustrated in Figure 5a. First, based on the nanomorphology of the precursor, the sublimated ligand gas reacts with metal oxide and forms a thin coating of MOF covering the surface of the template (stage 1, surface reaction). Prolonging the reaction time, the ligand gas penetrates through the nanopore of MOF coating and continues to react with the inner metal oxide. When the coating thickens, the outer MOF coating tends to grow on specific orientation and forms small crystal seeds (stage 2, seed formation). The vaporized ligand gas prefers to react with the exposed metal oxide, therefore there are no crystal seeds at surface reaction stage. When the coating thickens to a certain extent, solid-state phase transformation occurs in the product. Finally, the homogeneous nucleation seeds form driven by the undercooling through metal/oxygen atom migration and single bonds' rotation of BDC ligand. As the reaction proceeds, the seeds finally grow into big crystals until the metal oxide is thoroughly consumed (stage 3, crystal growth).
After the reaction, the NiO-BDC, NiO-BTC, NiO-2MIM, FeO-BDC, FeO-BTC, CuO-BDC, CuO-BTC, and CuO-2MIM can be successfully synthesized. The morphologies, elemental composition, and distribution at distinct stages were obtained by SEM and EDX (Figure 5c–k; Figures S33–S46, Supporting Information), providing the evidence for the growth mechanism. Surprisingly, polyoxometalates like \(\mathrm{CoMoO}_{4}\) could also be transformed by 2-MIM, and the product presents single-crystal nature with the characteristic truncated rhombic dodecahedron morphology (Figure S47, Supporting Information). Of course, besides BDC, BTC, and 2-MIM, SVPT is suitable for other ligands as well. But one thing to note is that the sublimation point or melting point of the ligand (phase change temperature, from solid phase to vapor phase or liquid phase) should not be higher than the thermal decomposition temperature. If so, the pressure of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere should be modulated to decrease the phase change temperature, according to the theory of ternary phase diagram.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, for the first time, we demonstrated an effective strategy to transform metal oxides, metal composite oxides, hydroxides, layered double hydroxides, and polyoxometalates into MOF materials by SVPT. For application on OER, an electrocatalyst of 7.5-1h LDH-MOF@NF with vertically aligned nanosheet arrays morphology was fabricated and displayed outstanding OER activity in alkaline media. Significantly, the LDH-MOF@NF catalyst exhibits high electrochemical surface area, fast charge and mass transfer kinetics, and long-term catalytic stability. The elaborately designed contrast of molecular structure between NiFe-based MOFs allows to explore the origin of the high OER activity: the modulation of NiFe metal center's electronic structure that facilitates the electrochemical reaction. Therefore, apart from the binder-free vertically aligned structure with large specific surface area, that is also the reason why LDH is the preferred template. Furthermore, the universality of this strategy was studied by reacting different kinds of metal oxides with distinct ligands, revealing the growth mechanism
of MOF crystals for future research. In brief, based on these mechanisms, we overcame the shortcomings of solution-based strategies to fabricate template-directed MOFs and developed the species of MOFs synthesized by vapor-phase reaction. The advantages of perfect precursor morphology inheritance can greatly increase the catalytic area, thereby effectively improving the catalytic performance of materials for OER. We believe that through changing the precursors and ligands, the demonstrated SVPT strategy may inspire the exploration and fabrication of more species of MOFs and applied in many more fields.
